WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.659 --> 00:00:11.893
&gt;&gt;&gt; All right.

00:00:12.026 --> 00:00:12.493
Last week an artificial

00:00:12.627 --> 00:00:14.428
intelligence company opened ai

00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:19.667
unveiled chatgpt4.

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.736
The ai language tool that's

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:23.671
frightening people around the

00:00:23.671 --> 00:00:27.675
wool, gpt4 that can analyze

00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:29.277
thousands of words and text that

00:00:29.677 --> 00:00:31.345
can analyze everything, coding,

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:33.848
to passing standardized tests

00:00:33.981 --> 00:00:36.417
and it can even write lawsuits.

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:38.719
Ai technology is developing at a

00:00:38.853 --> 00:00:40.922
dizzying pace and able to

00:00:41.055 --> 00:00:43.190
re-create voices as Antonio

00:00:43.324 --> 00:00:44.525
Sullivan found out and we showed

00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:47.228
it to you last weekend by using

00:00:47.361 --> 00:00:48.696
one to simulate his own voice in

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:52.266
a phone call with his parents.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:52.833
&gt;&gt; How are you doing?

00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:56.037
&gt;&gt; Hi, dad.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.240
How are you?

00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:00.207
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:01:00.341 --> 00:01:00.575
Yourself?

00:01:00.708 --> 00:01:01.943
&gt;&gt; Just finished shooting our

00:01:02.076 --> 00:01:02.410
story here.

00:01:02.543 --> 00:01:03.210
I'm going to the airport in a

00:01:03.344 --> 00:01:05.713
while.

00:01:05.813 --> 00:01:10.585
&gt;&gt; Oh, you're going back?

00:01:10.718 --> 00:01:13.988
&gt;&gt; The plane this weekend.

00:01:14.121 --> 00:01:14.789
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:01:14.922 --> 00:01:16.958
Well, it may seem like fun and

00:01:17.091 --> 00:01:17.258
games.

00:01:17.391 --> 00:01:18.693
Ai technology can even be used

00:01:18.826 --> 00:01:21.295
to create so-called deep fakes.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:23.731
That's not so funny which are

00:01:23.731 --> 00:01:25.232
digital masks that can replicate

00:01:25.366 --> 00:01:26.801
the look of just about anyone

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:31.839
like this.

00:01:31.973 --> 00:01:33.374
&gt;&gt; If you're a pretty good

00:01:33.741 --> 00:01:34.108
climber I want to see you climb

00:01:34.241 --> 00:01:38.145
this.

00:01:38.279 --> 00:01:41.282
Not easy to do.

00:01:41.415 --> 00:01:42.416
Discipline and hard work.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.185
I don't know what I'm talking

00:01:44.318 --> 00:01:44.485
about.

00:01:44.619 --> 00:01:44.885
It's crazy!

00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:46.487
&gt;&gt; Remember that?

00:01:46.621 --> 00:01:47.989
That was not Tom Cruise even

00:01:48.122 --> 00:01:49.757
though it looked like him.

00:01:49.824 --> 00:01:51.192
Some are now raising alarms

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:52.293
about how these advances could

00:01:52.426 --> 00:01:55.162
be used to create and spread

00:01:55.296 --> 00:01:55.763
misinformation.

00:01:55.763 --> 00:02:00.201
Our next guest is an ai expert

00:02:00.334 --> 00:02:05.773
and amerity us professor of

00:02:05.773 --> 00:02:07.241
psychology and author of

00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:08.943
rebooting ai, building

00:02:09.076 --> 00:02:11.045
artificial intelligence we can

00:02:11.178 --> 00:02:11.779
trust.

00:02:11.779 --> 00:02:13.014
Welcome, Dr. Gary Marcus.

00:02:13.147 --> 00:02:14.115
Good to see you.

00:02:14.248 --> 00:02:16.083
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having me.

00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:17.918
Doctor, you know more about this

00:02:18.052 --> 00:02:19.220
technology than most.

00:02:19.353 --> 00:02:21.856
You've even developed your own

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:22.223
versions.

00:02:22.356 --> 00:02:24.191
So for all of us who are just

00:02:24.325 --> 00:02:25.059
learning about this whole world

00:02:25.192 --> 00:02:29.130
of ai, how does it work in its

00:02:29.263 --> 00:02:30.431
current form and why is it that

00:02:30.564 --> 00:02:33.934
you are concerned about kind of

00:02:34.068 --> 00:02:34.402
the next level?

00:02:34.535 --> 00:02:34.969
What can happen next?

00:02:35.102 --> 00:02:37.838
&gt;&gt; I think the first thing to

00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:39.306
understand is it's not as

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:40.408
intelligence as it looks.

00:02:40.541 --> 00:02:43.244
It's mimicking a huge database

00:02:43.377 --> 00:02:44.879
of things that it's seen before

00:02:45.012 --> 00:02:47.448
and it is so huge that an

00:02:47.815 --> 00:02:49.216
ordinary human being probably

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.819
can't imagine how huge it is and

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:54.355
it's billions of texts and what

00:02:54.488 --> 00:02:58.292
it's doing is it's prestiging

00:02:58.426 --> 00:03:00.461
them, it's piecing together

00:03:00.594 --> 00:03:02.630
weird things.

00:03:02.763 --> 00:03:05.099
It blamed the silicon valley

00:03:05.232 --> 00:03:15.843
bank on gpt5 and it didn't

00:03:15.976 --> 00:03:17.111
exist.

00:03:17.244 --> 00:03:19.013
Even though it looks smart it's

00:03:19.146 --> 00:03:20.281
making stuff up.

00:03:20.414 --> 00:03:23.084
&gt;&gt; Maybe it shouldn't be called

00:03:23.217 --> 00:03:24.452
artificial intelligence, but

00:03:24.585 --> 00:03:25.086
something else.

00:03:25.219 --> 00:03:28.856
You wrote an article in "The

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:29.223
atlantic."

00:03:29.356 --> 00:03:31.459
We talked about how bad actors

00:03:31.859 --> 00:03:34.628
can manipulate the powers of the

00:03:34.762 --> 00:03:37.498
ai tools to spread

00:03:37.865 --> 00:03:37.965
misinformation.

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:39.867
I guess, what are the degrees of

00:03:39.867 --> 00:03:41.001
your worries about this?

00:03:41.135 --> 00:03:42.737
Is there going to be a point

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.638
where it's going to be difficult

00:03:44.772 --> 00:03:47.875
to differentiate real thing from

00:03:47.875 --> 00:03:50.344
ai generated if we're not there

00:03:50.478 --> 00:03:50.678
already?

00:03:50.811 --> 00:03:52.613
&gt;&gt; We are very close to that

00:03:52.747 --> 00:03:55.082
point right now, and part of the

00:03:55.216 --> 00:03:57.518
concern that bad actors can

00:03:57.885 --> 00:03:59.053
deliberately use this to miss

00:03:59.186 --> 00:03:59.887
inform people.

00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:01.889
It's one thing to have fun with

00:04:01.889 --> 00:04:03.124
Tom Cruise and having him say

00:04:03.257 --> 00:04:05.092
something wacky, but you can

00:04:05.226 --> 00:04:09.263
imagine troll farms trying to

00:04:09.396 --> 00:04:09.997
influence our elections and my

00:04:10.131 --> 00:04:11.298
single biggest fear is that that

00:04:11.432 --> 00:04:13.367
people will disrupt democracy by

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:15.569
making an atmosphere where we

00:04:15.903 --> 00:04:16.504
can't trust anything.

00:04:16.637 --> 00:04:17.905
Where when you look at Twitter

00:04:18.038 --> 00:04:19.974
and you look at Facebook, you

00:04:20.107 --> 00:04:21.208
don't know what's true anymore.

00:04:21.342 --> 00:04:21.909
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:04:21.909 --> 00:04:23.210
&gt;&gt; The sheer volume that you can

00:04:23.344 --> 00:04:25.412
make with these machines.

00:04:25.546 --> 00:04:27.915
You can make millions and

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:30.751
billions of misinformation a day

00:04:30.885 --> 00:04:32.353
with your own alternative set of

00:04:32.486 --> 00:04:35.923
facts are and it's plausible and

00:04:35.923 --> 00:04:37.191
it rz references and data and

00:04:37.324 --> 00:04:39.393
most humans won't be able to

00:04:39.527 --> 00:04:41.929
tell the difference and a lot of

00:04:41.929 --> 00:04:42.730
people can't trust anything.

00:04:42.863 --> 00:04:44.165
&gt;&gt; That's my biggest worry.

00:04:44.298 --> 00:04:48.402
We just came off of it.

00:04:48.536 --> 00:04:50.838
A lot of people are stuck.

00:04:50.971 --> 00:04:53.274
They've used the terminology of

00:04:53.407 --> 00:04:55.943
fake news and then people

00:04:56.043 --> 00:04:56.610
believe it and then you have

00:04:56.744 --> 00:04:58.379
this forum, these tools that can

00:04:58.512 --> 00:05:00.714
help that happen and look more

00:05:00.848 --> 00:05:02.883
believable and people can't

00:05:03.017 --> 00:05:03.951
discern the difference between

00:05:04.018 --> 00:05:04.752
real and fake.

00:05:04.885 --> 00:05:07.221
I feel like we may have run into

00:05:07.354 --> 00:05:11.959
this jurassic park moment where

00:05:11.959 --> 00:05:13.127
these ai creators are so

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:14.261
preoccupied with whether they

00:05:14.395 --> 00:05:16.497
could, they didn't stop to think

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.967
if we really should.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.070
&gt;&gt; I think that's exactly right.

00:05:23.204 --> 00:05:25.973
I wrote another essay, the

00:05:25.973 --> 00:05:28.142
jurassic park moment of ai and

00:05:28.275 --> 00:05:29.243
quoted that bit.

00:05:31.178 --> 00:05:34.348
I quoted Jeff blume and how that

00:05:34.481 --> 00:05:36.917
accurate is.

00:05:37.051 --> 00:05:38.619
In terms of the uses of

00:05:38.752 --> 00:05:40.154
misinformation and in terms of

00:05:40.287 --> 00:05:41.188
accidental misinformation around

00:05:41.322 --> 00:05:42.690
health and ultimately people

